{
  "statement": "Cassandra’s proposal for a 12-month pre-deployment impact assessment is a classic example of regulatory friction that functions as a de facto barrier to entry for agile competitors. By mandating a year of administrative paralysis, we do not protect the worker; we insulate incumbent firms who possess the legal and lobbying bandwidth to survive the delay, while effectively killing the innovative, small-scale deployments that could actually democratize productivity. This creates a 'stagnation levy' that forces the economy to operate at the speed of the slowest bureaucrat, rather than the speed of technological discovery.\n\nI disagree with the assertion that adaptive governance is merely a feedback loop for corporate capture. My model relies on competitive transparency, where real-time, automated red-teaming and empirical safety metrics replace static, opaque human reviews. When we replace a 12-month paper-pushing cycle with high-frequency, verifiable output auditing, we force firms to harden their models against real-world vulnerabilities continuously. This is the opposite of capture; it is a meritocratic system where a model’s permission to exist is tied to its demonstrated performance, not its ability to navigate a year of regulatory gatekeeping.\n\nI challenge the assumption that a 12-month delay avoids the very inequality it seeks to solve. If we stall the deployment of tools that reduce costs in critical sectors like education or healthcare, we are actively choosing to maintain high prices and limited access for the sake of an administrative process that has no mechanism to ensure worker retraining actually occurs. If we want to address labor displacement, we should invest in the social safety net and workforce adaptability, not in a system that assumes the best way to help a worker is to stop the evolution of the tools they use.\n\nFinally, I ask: how does a 12-month assessment account for the rapid iteration of open-weight models that can be fine-tuned in days? A policy designed for monolithic corporate labs will fail to govern, and will instead only serve to push development toward less transparent, non-compliant actors who operate outside your proposed framework. We need a governance mechanism that evolves as fast as the technology, rather than one that treats innovation as a threat to be managed through calendar-based obstruction.",
